# React Component Conventions — public-v2

React 19, TypeScript 5.9, Vite 8. Apply to every file in `public-v2/src/`.

## Component pattern — **named export** (not default)

**This repo uses named exports for pages**, consumed via `React.lazy(() => import('./pages/X').then(m => ({ default: m.X })))`. Don't change this pattern.

```tsx
import { type ReactNode } from 'react';

interface LoanCardProps {
  loanId: string;
  amount: number;
  status: 'pending' | 'approved' | 'rejected' | 'manual_review';
  onCancel?: () => void;
  children?: ReactNode;
}

export function LoanCard({ loanId, amount, status, onCancel, children }: LoanCardProps) {
  return (
    <div className="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 shadow-sm border border-teal-900/10">
      {/* ... */}
    </div>
  );
}
```

**Why named export:**
- `App.tsx` uses `.then(m => ({ default: m.X }))` to pick up named exports for lazy loading
- Default exports get renamed unpredictably in bundlers
- Easier to `Cmd+Click` navigate in Cursor

**No `React.FC`** — adds implicit `children`, breaks generics.

## Props rules

- Required props first, optional (`?`) last
- Boolean props default to `false` (design so unset = default behavior)
- Never spread props blindly — destructure explicitly
- Callbacks: `onSomething`, not `handleSomething`
- Discriminated unions over multiple booleans:

```tsx
// Bad
interface BadProps { isPrimary?: boolean; isSecondary?: boolean }

// Good
interface GoodProps { variant: 'primary' | 'secondary' | 'ghost' }
```

## Auth — use the existing `useAuth`

There's already a robust `useAuth` in `src/hooks/useAuth.ts` with:
- Cached token → force-refreshed token → Firestore fallback for role resolution
- Handles `employers/{uid}` AND `users/{uid}` documents
- Returns `{ user, role, loading }`

**Don't rewrite it.** Import and use:

```tsx
import { useAuth } from '@/hooks/useAuth';

function MyComponent() {
  const { user, role, loading } = useAuth();
  if (loading) return <Spinner />;
  if (!user) return <Navigate to="/login" replace />;
  // ...
}
```

Role is one of: `'employee' | 'employer_admin' | 'ops' | 'admin' | 'super_admin' | null`. Route protection is via `<RouteGuard allowedRoles={[...]}>` in `App.tsx` — use that pattern, not inline checks.

## Firestore data fetching

**No custom hook exists yet** for loans/employees/etc. Create one per doc type:

```tsx
// src/hooks/useLoan.ts
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { doc, onSnapshot } from 'firebase/firestore';
import { db } from '@/lib/firebase';

interface Loan {
  id: string;
  employeeId: string;
  amount: number;
  status: 'pending' | 'approved' | 'rejected' | 'manual_review';
  // ... extend as you find real fields
}

export function useLoan(loanId: string | null) {
  const [loan, setLoan] = useState<Loan | null>(null);
  const [error, setError] = useState<Error | null>(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!loanId) { setLoading(false); return; }
    const unsub = onSnapshot(
      doc(db, 'loans', loanId),
      (snap) => {
        setLoan(snap.exists() ? ({ id: snap.id, ...snap.data() } as Loan) : null);
        setLoading(false);
      },
      (err) => { setError(err); setLoading(false); },
    );
    return unsub;
  }, [loanId]);

  return { loan, loading, error };
}
```

**Never** call `getDoc` or `onSnapshot` directly in component body. Always through a hook.

## Cloud Function calls

Import from `firebase/functions`:

```tsx
import { getFunctions, httpsCallable, type HttpsError } from 'firebase/functions';

const functions = getFunctions();  // default region us-central1

const requestLoan = httpsCallable<
  { amount: number; termDays: number; termsAccepted: boolean },
  { loanId: string; status: string }
>(functions, 'requestLoan');

async function submit() {
  try {
    const { data } = await requestLoan({ amount: 5000, termDays: 30, termsAccepted: true });
    // data is typed
  } catch (err) {
    // err is FirebaseError with .code and .message
  }
}
```

Never fetch CF endpoints via `fetch()` — lose auth + App Check + type safety.

## Error mapping

**No `src/lib/errors.ts` exists yet.** Create one when needed:

```tsx
// src/lib/errors.ts
interface MaybeHttpsError {
  code?: string;
  message?: string;
}

export function friendlyError(err: unknown): string {
  const e = err as MaybeHttpsError;
  if (!e?.code) return 'Algo salió mal. Intenta de nuevo.';
  switch (e.code) {
    case 'functions/unauthenticated':
    case 'unauthenticated':
      return 'Tu sesión expiró. Inicia sesión de nuevo.';
    case 'functions/permission-denied':
    case 'permission-denied':
      return 'No tienes permiso para esta acción.';
    case 'functions/resource-exhausted':
    case 'resource-exhausted':
      return 'Demasiadas solicitudes. Espera un minuto.';
    case 'functions/not-found':
    case 'not-found':
      return 'No encontramos lo que buscas.';
    case 'functions/already-exists':
    case 'already-exists':
      return 'Este recurso ya existe.';
    case 'functions/invalid-argument':
    case 'invalid-argument':
      return e.message || 'Los datos enviados no son válidos.';
    case 'functions/failed-precondition':
    case 'failed-precondition':
      return e.message || 'No se puede completar esta acción en este estado.';
    default:
      return 'Algo salió mal. Intenta de nuevo.';
  }
}
```

## State management

- **Local:** `useState` / `useReducer`
- **Route state:** URL params via `useParams`, `useSearchParams` — don't sync to React state
- **Server state:** Firestore `onSnapshot` (real-time) or one-off `getDoc`
- **Global UI state:** React Context (sparingly — `AuthProvider` is the main example)
- **Forms:** controlled `useState` — no form library installed

## TypeScript rules

- **Strict mode on** — respect it
- **`any` is banned** — use `unknown` + narrowing, or define proper types
- **Prefer `type` over `interface`** unless declaration merging needed
- **Use `satisfies`** over type assertions where possible
- **No `@ts-ignore`** — use `@ts-expect-error` with a one-line reason, or fix

## Styling

**Tailwind v4 utility classes** for 90% of cases. Use the real tokens (`bg-teal-900`, `text-gold-500`) — NOT `bg-vida-teal` (doesn't exist).

**Legacy CSS classes** (in `styles/legacy.css`) — use when extending legacy components (`.hero`, `.benefits-bar`, `.wrap`, `.rv`, `.auth-btn`, `.admin-*`).

**Class composition** — no `clsx` installed. Use template literals:

```tsx
<button className={`h-11 px-5 rounded-lg font-semibold transition-colors ${
  variant === 'primary' ? 'bg-teal-900 hover:bg-teal-800 text-white' :
  variant === 'secondary' ? 'border border-teal-900 text-teal-900 hover:bg-teal-900/5' :
  'text-teal-900 hover:underline'
} ${disabled ? 'opacity-50 cursor-not-allowed' : ''}`}>
```

Or extract a `cn()` helper if the component has 5+ conditional classes (3-line helper, no new dep).

## Imports

Order:

```tsx
// 1. External — alphabetical
import { Suspense, useState } from 'react';
import { Navigate } from 'react-router-dom';
import { useTranslation } from 'react-i18next';

// 2. Internal via @ alias — alphabetical
import { friendlyError } from '@/lib/errors';
import { db } from '@/lib/firebase';
import { useAuth } from '@/hooks/useAuth';

// 3. Relative — alphabetical
import { LoanAmountSlider } from './LoanAmountSlider';

// 4. Type-only — last
import type { Loan } from '@/types/loan';
```

The `@/` alias IS configured in `vite.config.ts` and `tsconfig.app.json`. Use it.

## i18n — use `useTranslation`

All user-facing strings go through i18next:

```tsx
import { useTranslation } from 'react-i18next';

function HeroSection() {
  const { t } = useTranslation();
  return (
    <div>
      <h1 dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: t('hero_h1') }} />
      <p>{t('hero_sub')}</p>
      <button>{t('hero_cta_employee')}</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

**Flat keys, not nested.** The convention in `es.json` is: `nav_employers`, `hero_h1`, `chip_24hrs` — snake_case, prefix-first. New keys follow this pattern.

**Add to both `es.json` AND `en.json`** in the same PR — don't split.

Some keys use `<em>` tags inside values (e.g. `"hero_h1": "Tu respaldo <em>financiero</em> integrado."`) — those are rendered via `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`. This is **existing pattern**, not preferred — but don't disrupt it for a single fix.

## Performance

- **Routes lazy-loaded** via `React.lazy` in `App.tsx` — maintain this pattern for new pages
- **Vendor chunks** pre-configured in `vite.config.ts`: react, react-dom, firebase, i18n split out. New heavy deps need chunking entries added
- **`useMemo` / `useCallback`** — use sparingly; profile before optimizing
- **Images:** `loading="lazy"` below fold, explicit `width` + `height`
- **Lists > 50 items:** virtualize (no library installed — propose `@tanstack/react-virtual` if needed)

## Error handling

Every async action handles the error path. Wrap CF calls:

```tsx
async function handleSubmit() {
  setSubmitting(true);
  try {
    await requestLoan(formData);
    // success feedback via toast or navigation
  } catch (err) {
    setError(friendlyError(err));
  } finally {
    setSubmitting(false);
  }
}
```

The app has a `<ErrorBoundary>` wrapping `<AuthProvider>` in `App.tsx` (global), plus per-portal boundaries wrapping each Layout (marketing, employee, employer, admin) and standalone routes (`/login`, `/onboarding`, `/*`). A crash in one lazy-loaded page degrades that route only — rest of the shell stays usable. Don't remove the wrapping.

## Anti-patterns to reject

- `React.FC` / `React.FunctionComponent`
- Default exports on pages (breaks the lazy-load pattern in App.tsx)
- Direct `fetch()` to CF endpoints — use `httpsCallable`
- `getDoc` / `onSnapshot` directly in component body — extract to a hook
- `any`, `object`, `Function` types
- `useEffect` without cleanup on subscriptions
- Components > 250 lines — split
- Props interfaces > 10 properties — component doing too much
- Prop drilling past 2 levels — context or restructure
- Mutating state (`array.push(x)` instead of `setArray([...array, x])`)
- Raw `localStorage.*` — use `@/lib/safeStorage` (`safeGetItem`, `safeSetItem`, `safeRemoveItem`) instead. Raw calls throw in Safari private mode and Firefox resist-fingerprinting.
